    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 160006\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n455 PM PDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated thunderstorms will be possible across Mohave County\n  Wednesday afternoon and evening, with gusty winds and localized heavy\n  rainfall possible. \n\n* Elsewhere, breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts will continue \n  through the week with dry conditions and near-normal \n  temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through early next week.\n\nDespite respectable northward moisture advection this afternoon,\nwarm mid-level temperatures are keeping the region capped and\nstable with some just some scattered cumulus fields across our\neastern zones. Surface dewpoints have climbed into the low to mid\n50s across southern Nevada and northwest Arizona, and even into\nthe mid-60s along the Colorado River Valley. As a closed low drops\ninto Northern California and the Great Basin on Wednesday,\nnorthward moisture transport will continue and a few storms may \nfire up across Mohave County during the afternoon and evening \nhours as favorable jet dynamics help overcome the persistent \ncapping. Storms should tend to move swiftly thanks to elevated \nwind shear in place, but training storms will have the potential \nfor localized flooding, and the enhanced shear could result in a \nfew stronger storms capable of small hail and strong wind gusts. \n\nElsewhere, dry conditions are expected to continue as dry\nsouthwesterly flow keeps monsoonal moisture focused further east\ninto Arizona. Daily gusty winds can be expected with afternoon\ngusts of 25 to 35 mph expected through Wednesday evening, trending\nslightly weaker Thursday. The flow pattern will flatten out over\nthe weekend with wind speeds easing area wide and dry and\nseasonable weather continuing. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...\nWinds struggled this afternoon to shift from SE to SSW and may do \nthat again tomorrow as an area of low pressure in the Sierra helps \nkeep winds backed to the SE. For the rest of the evening, SSW winds \n15 kt with gusts approaching 30 kt continue through 03z. Frequent \ngusts drop off into the night while S winds diminish and remain at \nor below 10 kt. Similar pattern expected again on Wednesday with SE-\nSSE winds increasing around 16z, switching to the S-SW 20-22z with \nmax gusts nearing 30 kt again. Lower confidence does exists in the \nexact timing of the SE to SW shift. Watching the potential for \nisolated storms tomorrow afternoon and evening over Mohave County, \nwhich could send outflow boundaries towards the Las Vegas Valley. \nNot including anything in the official TAF yet, but it will be \nsomething to watch out for tomorrow. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Daily wind patterns \nexpected at most sites through the TAF period. Strong SE-S winds 10-\n15 kt with gusts 20-30 kt continue for most sites through sunset. \nKDAG is the exception as strong west winds 10-18kt with higher gusts \ncontinue through around 07z then diminishing to around 10 kt through \nWednesday morning. Winds at KIFP and KEED should diminish somewhat \nafter sunset but confidence remains higher that elevated winds may \npersist overnight with isolated gusts 15-20kt. Isolated storms are \nexpected, mainly over northwestern Arizona, this evening then again \nWednesday afternoon. There is a slight threat for outflow from \nstorms to impact terminals in the CRV and possibly Las Vegas Valley \nWednesday afternoon and evening, but at this moment confidence is \nfairly low. Otherwise, VFR conditions prevail with no operationally \nsignificant cloud cover elsewhere.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Outler\nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
